.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B xstdio.h
library provides an extended set of I/O functions that enhance
POSIX and ISO C standard I/O functionality with additional features
such as Unicode-aware formatted output, full-file reading, descriptor
helpers, and dynamically allocated string builders.

Functions that allocate memory return dynamically allocated buffers
that must be released by the caller using
.BR free (3).

.SS FILE STREAM LOCKING

.BR "void xflockfile(FILE *fp);"

Locks the
.B FILE *
stream for the calling thread.

On Windows, this function uses
.BR _lock_file ().
On POSIX systems, it uses
.BR flockfile ().

The function provides a portable interface for explicitly locking a
.B FILE *
stream before performing a sequence of operations that must be protected
from concurrent access.

The lock remains held until the corresponding unlock operation is called.

.SS FLAG-BASED FILE OPENING

.BR "FILE *fopenm(const char *xrestrict filename, int oflag, ...);"

Opens a filename using
.BR open (3)-style
flags and returns a standard C
.B FILE *
stream.

The function translates the supported flags into an appropriate
.BR fopen (3)
mode and opens the file in binary mode.

Supported flags include:

.IP (bu 2
.BR O_RDONLY
.IP (bu 2
.BR O_WRONLY
.IP (bu 2
.BR O_RDWR
.IP (bu 2
.BR O_APPEND
.IP (bu 2
.BR O_BINARY

.SS MODE MAPPING

The following flag combinations are supported:

.TS
tab(@);
l l.
Flags@fopen() mode
O_RDONLY@"rb"
O_WRONLY@"wb"
O_WRONLY | O_APPEND@"ab"
O_RDWR@"wb+"
O_RDWR | O_APPEND@"ab+"
.TE

On success,
.BR fopenm ()
returns a
.B FILE *
stream.

On failure, it returns
.BR NULL .

If an invalid access-mode combination is supplied,
.BR errno
is set to
.BR EINVAL .

.BR O_EXCL
cannot be safely emulated using
.BR fopen (3)
and is not supported by this implementation.

.SS wfopenm()

.BR "FILE *wfopenm(const wchar_t *xrestrict filename, int oflag, ...);"

Wide-character equivalent of
.BR fopenm ().

On Windows,
.BR wfopenm ()
uses
.BR _wfopen ()
with a wide-character filename.

On POSIX systems, the filename is converted to the platform's
multibyte representation before opening the file.

The same
.BR oflag
values and mode mapping used by
.BR fopenm ()
apply.

.SS EXAMPLE

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include "xstdio.h"

FILE *fp = fopenm("data.bin", O_RDWR | O_APPEND);

if (!fp) {
perror("fopenm");
return 1;
}

fputs("data\n", fp);
fclose(fp);
.fi

A wide-character filename may be opened with
.BR wfopenm ():

.nf
FILE *fp = wfopenm(L"data.bin", O_RDWR | O_APPEND);

if (!fp) {
perror("wfopenm");
return 1;
}

fputs("data\n", fp);
fclose(fp);
.fi

Both functions provide a flag-based interface while returning a standard
.B FILE *
stream suitable for use with normal C stdio functions.

.SS DESCRIPTOR OUTPUT

.BR "int fdputs(const char *s, int fd);"

Writes the string
.I s
to the file descriptor
.IR fd .

Returns zero on success and
.BR -1
on failure.

.SS CONVERT FILE DESCRIPTOR TO FILE STREAM

.BR "FILE *fdno(int fd);"

Creates a
.B FILE *
stream from the file descriptor
.IR fd .

The descriptor is duplicated before being associated with the resulting
stream, allowing the returned stream to be closed independently of the
original descriptor.

.BR "FILE *fdno_unlocked(int fd);"

Creates a
.B FILE *
stream from the file descriptor
.IR fd
without applying the stream locking operation used by the corresponding
locked interface.

The exact locking behavior is implementation-dependent and should not
be confused with the lifetime or ownership of the underlying file
descriptor.

.SS FULL FILE READ

.BR "size_t fpsize(FILE *fp);"

Retrieves the size of the file associated with the
.B FILE *
stream
.IR fp .

The original file position is preserved.

.BR "void *furead(FILE *fp, size_t *out_size);"

Reads the entire contents of a
.B FILE *
stream into dynamically allocated memory.

The original file position is preserved.

On success, the function returns a pointer to the allocated buffer and
stores the number of bytes read in
.IR *out_size .

.BR "size_t fdsize(int fd);"

Retrieves the size of the file associated with the file descriptor
.IR fd .

The function may temporarily change the descriptor's file position
while determining the size and restores the original position before
returning.

.BR "void *fduread(int fd, size_t *out_size);"

Reads all data from the file descriptor
.IR fd
until end-of-file.

On success, the function returns a dynamically allocated buffer and
stores the number of bytes read in
.IR *out_size .

.SS DYNAMIC STRING BUILDERS

.BR "char *vcprintf(const char *fmt, va_list ap);"

Formats a string according to
.I fmt
and returns a newly allocated string.

.BR "char *cprintf(const char *fmt, ...);"

Formats a string according to
.I fmt
and returns a dynamically allocated string.

.BR "wchar_t *vwcprintf(const wchar_t *fmt, va_list ap);"

Formats a wide-character string according to
.I fmt
and returns a newly allocated wide-character string.

.BR "wchar_t *wcprintf(const wchar_t *fmt, ...);"

Formats a wide-character string according to
.I fmt
and returns a dynamically allocated wide-character string.

.BR "char *vwccprintf(const wchar_t *fmt, va_list ap);"

Formats a wide-character string and converts the resulting string to
UTF-8.

.BR "char *wccprintf(const wchar_t *fmt, ...);"

Formats a wide-character string and converts the resulting string to
UTF-8.

.SS UNICODE FILE OPERATIONS

.BR "FILE *wfopen(const wchar_t *filename, const wchar_t *modes);"

Unicode-aware version of
.BR fopen (3).

On Windows, it uses
.BR _wfopen ().
On POSIX systems, the wide-character filename is converted to the
platform's multibyte representation before calling
.BR fopen (3).

.BR "int xremove(const char *fmt, ...);"

Formats a UTF-8 file path and removes the resulting file.

.BR "int wremove(const wchar_t *s);"

Removes a file using a wide-character filename.

.BR "int xwremove(const wchar_t *fmt, ...);"

Formats a wide-character file path and removes the resulting file.

.SS GET DIRECTORY OF CURRENT EXECUTABLE

.BR "char *getcurrentdirectory_size(size_t n);"

Retrieves the directory of the current executable in UTF-8 format,
using a buffer size of
.IR n .

.BR "char *getcurrentdirectory(void);"

Retrieves the directory of the current executable in UTF-8 format
using the default buffer size.

.BR "wchar_t *wgetcurrentdirectory_size(size_t n);"

Retrieves the directory of the current executable in wide-character
format, using a buffer size of
.IR n .

.BR "wchar_t *wgetcurrentdirectory(void);"

Retrieves the directory of the current executable in wide-character
format using the default buffer size.

.SH ERROR HANDLING

Most functions in
.B xstdio.h
set
.BR errno
on failure.

Common errors include:

.IP (bu 2
.BR ENOMEM
Memory allocation failure.

.IP (bu 2
.BR EINVAL
Invalid argument.

.IP (bu 2
.BR ENAMETOOLONG
Path name too long.

.IP (bu 2
.BR ENOENT
No such file or directory.

Functions that allocate memory generally return
.BR NULL
when an error occurs.

.SH MEMORY MANAGEMENT

Functions that return dynamically allocated memory require the caller
to release the returned buffer with
.BR free (3).

This includes, among others:

.IP (bu 2
.BR cprintf ()
.IP (bu 2
.BR wcprintf ()
.IP (bu 2
.BR furead ()
.IP (bu 2
.BR fduread ()
.IP (bu 2
.BR vwccprintf ()
.IP (bu 2
.BR wccprintf ()

.SH THREAD SAFETY

The standard C stdio functions generally provide implementation-defined
or implementation-supported internal synchronization.

.BR xflockfile ()
provides explicit stream locking when a sequence of operations must be
performed while holding the stream lock.

The caller is responsible for releasing an explicitly acquired stream
lock using the corresponding unlock operation.

.SH CROSS-PLATFORM NOTES

On Windows, functions such as
.BR wfopen ()
use Windows-specific APIs including
.BR _wfopen ()
and
.BR _lock_file ().

On POSIX systems, wide-character filenames are converted to the
platform's multibyte representation before being passed to functions
such as
.BR fopen (3).

